learnings:
  - 'The day''s whole method was: audit (team panel) → design → adversarial review → build → verify-state-directly → ship-per-round (build+lore+changelog+commit+push) → cut release → live-test-before-merge. It produced 3 releases without a regression and caught 3 would-be-false claims. This is the reusable loop for the next arc.'
  - Builders repeatedly truncated their final report on large multi-file edits (~90-110k tokens); the mitigation that worked every time was to verify state DIRECTLY (git diff + build + full test suite + a live smoke test) rather than trust the agent's report. Split big builds into new-file-heavy sub-rounds to dodge the limit.
  - Live integration testing against the REAL external system (the actual GitHub repo) caught a sync bug a mocked unit test could not (the mock accepted the bad email assignee). For surface/integration work in the renaissance, test against the real thing before merge.
